Real code:

public class Sleeper implements Runnable {

    @Override
    public void run() {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.out.println("error");
        }
    }
}

Four threads each run this Sleeper on a machine with ONE core. Roughly how long until all four are done, and why?,"12 seconds, because one core can only handle one sleep at a time","About 3 seconds. A sleeping thread is not using the core at all, so all four can wait at the same time even on a single core","3 seconds, but only because the JVM merges identical sleeps",Unpredictable - sleeping threads have no defined duration,,2,"This is the key difference from CPU work. Waiting needs no core, so waiting in parallel costs nothing. This is why threads help so much with network calls, file reads and database queries.",2

Real code:

public class Fibonacci implements Runnable {

    private int n;

    public Fibonacci(int n) {
        this.n = n;
    }

    private int getNthfib(int n) {
        if (n == 1 || n == 2) {
            return 1;
        }
        return getNthfib(n - 1) + getNthfib(n - 2);
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(getNthfib(n));
    }
}

// Main
int[] numbers = {6, 7, 8, 9};

for (int n : numbers) {
    Fibonacci task = new Fibonacci(n);
    Thread t = new Thread(task);
    t.start();
}

This is the fibonacci demo from class. It starts four threads for the numbers 6, 7, 8 and 9. Does this demo prove that threading made the work faster?,Yes - four threads means roughly four times faster,"No. getNthfib(9) is only 34, so each calculation takes microseconds. Creating a thread costs more than the work itself, so the timing shows nothing useful about speed","Yes, but only because fibonacci is recursive","No, because Runnable cannot be used for CPU work",,2,"The demo is good for showing thread creation and unpredictable output order. It is useless as a speed demo because the work is far too small. To show a real speedup the number has to be big enough to take real time, like fib(36) and up.",2

Real code:

public class Fibonacci implements Runnable {

    private int n;

    public Fibonacci(int n) {
        this.n = n;
    }

    private int getNthfib(int n) {
        if (n == 1 || n == 2) {
            return 1;
        }
        return getNthfib(n - 1) + getNthfib(n - 2);
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println(getNthfib(n));
    }
}

To turn the fibonacci example into an honest speed demo, what has to change?,Use extends Thread instead of implements Runnable,"Make n large enough that one calculation takes noticeable time (fib(36) and above), and time the whole batch with and without threads",Add a sleep inside getNthfib so the threads have time to start,Run it on a single core so the difference is clearer,,2,Adding a sleep would be dishonest here - it would turn a CPU test into a waiting test and prove something different. The fix is simply to make the CPU work big enough to measure.,2
